A least-privilege IAM policy names specific actions and specific resources, not wildcards. Compare an over-broad grant to a scoped one for a service that only needs to read from one storage bucket:

```json
// Over-broad: full admin access to every bucket in the account
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "s3:*",
  "Resource": "*"
}

// Least privilege: read-only, one bucket, one prefix
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
  "Resource": [
    "arn:aws:s3:::orders-export/reports/*",
    "arn:aws:s3:::orders-export"
  ]
}
```

The difference isn't stylistic. If the service holding the first policy is compromised, the attacker can read, write, or delete anything in every bucket in the account. If it's compromised while holding the second, the blast radius is exactly one read-only prefix — the policy itself is doing real containment work, before any incident response even begins.
